data: {"type":"response.reasoning_summary_text.done","summary_index":0,"item_id":"rs_02179466206690400000000000000000000ffffac151e26fe6553","output_index":0,"text":"We need produce candidate JSON and use tools compile_st, simulate_st, verify_st (max 3 each). Need think deeply about semantics.\n\nWe need define FB_Task with exact interface. Likely internal TON timers? Need implement controller with elapsed clock logic consistent with scan semantics, including dt_ms? Wait interface only TemperatureSensorInput and SetTemperature inputs. Task says \"Timer elapsed time is zero on the first eligible sampled invocation; dt_ms is elapsed wall time since previous invocation, not automatically a synthetic input to FB.\" Simulation test cases include optional dt_ms. How does FB see dt_ms? Standard library? Maybe use TIME/TON? In simulation, perhaps simulator advances time automatically and TON uses time. But no dt input. Need design with TON blocks using system time? Compilation ST. Simulation harness likely simulates with dt_ms provided and POSIX time? Need know PLC tools behavior. We can't inspect dirs. Could use TON timers. Requirements and LTL reference \"internal elapsed-time clock at 0 ms (ignoring dt_ms[0]); subsequent scans advance by dt_ms[k]\". A TON block's elapsed updates with scan cycles in CODESYS based on task interval. Simulator likely models TIME/TON? Tool simulate_st likely supports dt_ms. We need infer expected solution.\n\nWe can call compile/simulate. Need create candidate.json despite read-only sandbox? Tool maybe writes? User says save. Shell read-only prevents writing. apply_patch perhaps can write? Filesystem restricted read only. Maybe no apply_patch tool available actually only shell and PLC. We could use shell perhaps denied. But later. Main task needs final JSON object and save. The tool likely permits candidate output. Need obey. Let's inspect nothing else per instruction. We can use compile tools directly.\n\nDesign.\n\nInterface:\nFUNCTION_BLOCK FB_Task\nVAR_INPUT\n TemperatureSensorInput : REAL;\n SetTemperature : REAL;\nEND_VAR\nVAR_OUTPUT\n CurrentTemperature : REAL;\n SetTempDisplay : REAL;\n HeaterStatus : BOOL;\n OverheatProtection : BOOL;\n HeaterOutput : BOOL;\nEND_VAR\nVAR\n sensorFault, demand, heatLimitReached? timers...\nEND_VAR\n\nInitial outputs default false and 0 naturally. CODESYS initial values as declared.\n\nTiming:\n- Track continuous heating time. First energized scan elapsed zero. Need trip at >=5s continuous. \"cooldown MUST clear at first observed elapsed>=3s\" from trip. TON semantics: TON with IN true starts timing and Q after PT; on first scan elapsed is 0. Reading ElapsedTime after call maybe increments by time since previous. But scan semantics spec: dt_ms since previous invocation; at scan k=0 clock initialized zero ignoring dt0; subsequent scan advances by dt[k]. Timer elapsed likely applies dt from current invocation? Need define.\n\nCould implement explicit timer using TON or TIME and current scan time. There's no dt input, but CODESYS has system time functions e.g. `TIME()`? Standard library `TON` gets task cycle time implicitly. The evaluator's simulator likely passes dt_ms and supports timers. We need know tool contract. Could simulate to see expected case schema. simulate_st accepts source and cases arbitrary. We can create cases and tool returns outputs. We need seed tests ourselves. Could use compile only and verify. Formal verifier might handle timers if supported? Maybe not.\n\nPotential implementation with TON:\n\ntonHeat : TON;\ntonCool : TON;\n\nAt each scan:\nCurrentTemperature := TemperatureSensorInput;\nSetTempDisplay := SetTemperature;\nsensorFault := (TemperatureSensorInput < 0.0) OR (TemperatureSensorInput > 100.0);\n\nDetermine demand:\nif sensorFault then demand retained (freeze)\nelsif TemperatureSensorInput < SetTemperature then demand := TRUE;\nelsif TemperatureSensorInput > SetTemperature then demand := FALSE;\nelse retain.\n\nOverheat state:\nif OverheatProtection then\n  maybe cooldown timer expires -> clear\nelse if heatLimitReached -> trip\nend.\n\nNeed trip logic order, first energized sample elapsed zero, trip at elapsed>=5. Need heatLimitReached representing continuous energized time \"has reached\" 5s at current scan before final output. If heater was energized continuously and this scan reaches 5s, trip in same scan, so output off. At k trip scan, protection true and no heater. This means continuous energized previous scans until threshold, then this scan limit reached before output.\n\nTime semantics:\nAt each invocation, timer \"heated elapsed\" advances by dt[k] perhaps. At first energized scan elapsed zero (not dt[k]); next invocation advances by dt. So if dt each 1000ms:\nscan 0 demand true: energized first sample elapsed=0 -> output true.\nscan 1: elapsed=1000 -> output true.\nscan 2: 2000\n... scan 5: 5000 -> trip, output false, protection true, cooldown starts at 0? At scan 5 trip timestamp. Cooldown elapsed zero at trip scan. scan 6: +1000, etc. At scan 8: 3000 -> clear immediately and may restart same scan. Reheat at scan 8 with energized true; continuous heat elapsed? Sensor fault resets continuous heating time; does overheat trip reset it? Trip means continuous heating ended, so reset heat elapsed. If clear at scan 8 and demand, output can heat, heat first energized sample elapsed=0. Then would trip at scan 13. Is protection cooldown timer reset. Good.\n\nBut P10: \"G(protectPrev & cooldownDone -> !OverheatProtection)\" where cooldownDone at elapsed>=3s from trip. At scan 8 protectPrev true, cooldownDone true -> output OverheatProtection false. Fine.\nP6: G(heatLimitReached -> protection & !output). If at scan 8 cooldown clears and heat starts same scan, heatLimit false.\n\nHow implement elapsed in a portable FB? Use TON for heating: `HeatTimer(IN := heating_continuous, PT := T#5S)` but if trip in same scan at 5s, if we call timer before deciding output, with IN := output previous? Need avoid. Let's model internal state.\n\nOption `tonHeat : TON` fed with actual HeaterOutput? Call `tonHeat(IN := HeaterOutput, PT := T#5S)` at start. On first energized scan, previous output true from last scan. Then timer elapsed includes dt since previous invocation. If first energized scan current output false, IN false -> elapsed 0. Then set output true. Next scan call timer true: after elapsed dt,... at five sec Q. But we need evaluate protection before finalize output this scan. Could call tonHeat first based on prior HeatEnabled state, then detect Q, trip, set output false. At trip scan Q true. That works: the heater was on for previous scans and reaches limit now; current output forced off. Timer elapsed at Q >= PT.\n\nBut at fresh first energized scan, timer called with IN false and reset, then output true. elapsed 0. Next scan with 1s dt, Q false, output remains true. At 5th subsequent scan (after five intervals) Q true, trip. That is continuous energized time reaches 5 sec. Good. On trip, timer IN becomes false and resets next scan. During protection, output false. At cooldown completion same scan may restart; need call cooldown TON based on previous protection state at start, then if Q and cooldown done clear. If same scan start heating, heat timer had IN false and resets, so elapsed 0. Good.\n\nCooldown TON:\ntonCool(IN := OverheatProtection, PT := T#3S). On trip scan, at start protection false, timer reset (elapsed 0). After process set protection true but no tick this scan. Next scan timer IN true starts at elapsed maybe 0? Important semantics: TON when IN rises, timing starts with elapsed 0 on that invocation; it doesn't add previous dt because it wasn't active during previous interval. Actually standard TON updates elapsed based on time since last call only if IN was true during whole interval. At first scan IN false, second scan true -> elapsed 0. At 3 seconds after that, Q. Trip time was scan 5, cooldown starts scan 6 at elapsed 0, then scan 7=1s, scan8=2s, scan9=3s clear. But spec says cooldown elapsed at current scan clock = clock - protection_start >= 3s. protection_start = trip timestamp at scan 5. At scan 8 (3 sec later) should clear. Our TON starts at scan 6 and clears scan 9, one scan late. Need handle.\n\nCan compute cooldown elapsed as accumulated dt while protection active including dt at first active scan? The trip occurs at timestamp t5. At scan 6, clock - t5 = dt6. But \"cooldown elapsed\" should be current clock minus trip clock. At trip scan, elapsed zero. Next scan add dt[k]. So explicit integer/TIME accumulator is easier if dt is observable.\n\nBut if using TON timer set from trip scan? At trip scan, call cooldown timer after determining trip with IN true and it would start elapsed 0. Next scan add dt. Clears at scan8 after 3 increments (1000 each) perhaps. TON's first call true on trip scan: Depending simulator, elapsed becomes 0; yes. We need reorder: detect heat timeout from heat timer, set protection true, call cool timer(IN:=protection) same scan. Then next scan it accumulates dt from previous call. At scan 8 elapsed 3s. That might align. But standard TON update semantics likely based on `elapsed = elapsed + dt` when IN true at invocation? Need understand CODESYS TON implementation. Standard TON: if IN false reset; if IN true then elapsed = elapsed + time since last call, Q = elapsed >= PT. If rising edge from false to true at call, time since previous call maybe full dt? Yet typical PLC TON starts timing only from call where IN is TRUE, no elapsed until next cycle. The scan semantics given: \"Timer elapsed time is zero on the first eligible sampled invocation; dt_ms is elapsed wall time since the previous invocation\". This precisely implies first eligible invocation elapsed zero, next invocation dt. So TON behaves that way. If we call cooldown at trip scan true, that's first eligible invocation elapsed 0. Next scan +dt. At trip+3s scan elapsed 3s and clear. Good.\n\nBut if using a single TON call before logic, trip scan protection previous false => not eligible; then set protection after. Need call after setting protection in same scan to initialize. Or use a BOOL coolTimerStarted to call on trip. Could restructure.\n\nHeat timer first eligible invocation = first energized sample, elapsed 0. If call heat timer at beginning based on previous output, first energized sample is scan where output was false at beginning but becomes true. The first \"energized invocation\" according to spec is that scan, but timer IN false and elapsed 0. Next scan +dt. At scan where 5s reached, Q true. Good. We could call `heatTimer(IN := HeaterOutput, PT:=T#5S)` at beginning (using previous output). At scan 0 fresh output false => reset, then set true. At scan k=1, previous output true, dt maybe 1000, timer elapsed 1000. Is first energized sample elapsed 0? yes scan0. Fine. At scan k=5 elapsed 5000, Q true, trip and output off. Perfect.\n\nBut what if sensor fault resets continuous heating: at fault scan, at beginning timer IN previous HeaterOutput true, so timer would increment, then sensorFault sets output false. Timer doesn't reset until next scan. If fault persists next scan calls false resets. At fault onset, timer increments one interval but then reset; output off. On recovery, first energized sample starts anew. Fine. Yet requirement \"sensor fault immediately turns heat off and resets continuous-heating time.\" At fault scan output false and next invocation timer resets. Could call timer IN := HeaterOutput AND NOT sensorFault maybe but sensor fault is sampled current scan so use `heatTimer(IN := HeaterOutput AND NOT sensorFault, PT:=...)` at beginning. That resets same scan. But sensorFault computed previous scan or current? We can compute sensorFault first then call timer with current fault. The first scan input valid with output false resets anyway. At fault onset resets. Better.\n\nDuring overheat, heatTimer IN should false (output false) and reset. Fine.\n\nDemand behavior: ghost retained demand. At equality retain. On sensor fault demand freezes (prior). On fault onset, demand remains whatever. HeaterOutput must off. On recovery when input valid:\n- if actual < set -> demand true\n- if actual > set -> demand false\n- equal -> retain frozen demand (which may have been prior).\nInputs stable between timed samples assumed, irrelevant.\n\nProtection trigger: \"If heater works continuously for more than certain period ... overheat\". Interpreted threshold elapsed>=5s. Need based on continuous heater energized. The timer Q on current scan before finalization. But when protection active and cooldown clears at scan, heat can restart same scan; trip cannot happen because heat timer should reset during cooldown. If heatTimer at beginning has IN=HeaterOutput (false during protection) => false resets. Then restart current scan output true. Good.\n\nProtection clear logic:\nAt each scan:\n- Compute sensor fault and demand. Sensor fault overrides demand output but demand frozen.\n- Determine if protection should clear based on cooldown elapsed >=3s. Could use coolTimer Q.\n- If protection active:\n  - if coolTimer.Q then protection := FALSE (clear this scan)\n  - else remains true; output false regardless.\n- else:\n  - if heatTimer.Q then protection := TRUE; output false.\n  - else output := (valid & demand); maybe any conditions.\n\nNeed call timers correctly to achieve exact threshold.\n\nOrder and TON:\n1. CurrentTemperature := input; SetTempDisplay := set.\n2. sensorFault := ...\n3. update demand (freeze during fault).\n4. Call heat timer `heatTimer(IN := HeaterOutput AND NOT sensorFault, PT := T#5S);` using previous output. But if sensorFault true, resets. Maybe heater should be off immediately anyway.\n5. Call cooldown timer `coolTimer(IN := OverheatProtection, PT := T#3S);` based previous protection. On scan protection false at beginning, trip false. If we trip this scan, cooldown not initialized. Need after trip call `coolTimer(IN := TRUE)`? We can call after decision. Simpler: manage cooldown timer after protection changes:\n  - `cooldownTimer(IN := OverheatProtection, PT:=T#3S);` at end, after setting/tripping/clearing. On trip set true then call so elapsed zero. On active true increments each scan. On clear set false then call resets. This is good.\n  - But to determine clear based on cooldown, need know timer elapsed from previous call before updating. We can evaluate `cooldownTimer.Q` from last invocation; then at end call cooldown with new state. At first scan after trip, cooldownTimer.Q from trip scan's call at end was false (elapsed0). Determine protection remains true. At end call, adds dt, etc. At exactly 3s, cooldownTimer.Q true from previous invocation's end call? Let's timeline with call timings.\n\nIf cooldown called at end of each scan after processing, with IN := current protection:\nscan trip k=5: at process, old protection false; trip. End call IN true, elapsed = 0 (first eligible)\nscan 6: process checks previous Q false, remains. End call IN true => elapsed dt6 (1s)\nscan 7: check Q false, end call => 2s\nscan 8: check Q false (was 2s), end call =>3s, Q true\nscan 9: check Q true -> clear. That's one scan late relative to eval: at scan8 clock - trip clock=3s should clear.\n\nInstead call cooldown at beginning with current protection (which on trip scan is false), then on trip initialize second call? Could call twice same scan? TON invocation twice maybe increments? No, time between calls ~0. Simulator may not simulate exact wall time. Not good.\n\nNeed model with explicit sampling so process at scan sees accumulated dt correctly. Could call timers after processing with new state and use returned Q to clear immediately? At scan8 end call reaches Q true but outputs already set. Could after end call, if Q true and protection true, clear protection and recalc output in same scan. That works. But if trip same scan, Q is false at elapsed0, no clear. For an already active protection at scan8, at process we can set output false then call cooldown new state true -> Q true -> then clear protection and potentially energize. But then must avoid re-energizing based on stale heat timer Q (should be false due prior false). Also output final. This could work, but careful with P7: at scan before done remains true.\n\nAlternative compute cooldown time by running timer at start with prior protection and then if Q clear; trip initialized at end. Timeline:\nscan 5 trip: start cooldown IN false reset; trip; end call IN true elapsed0.\nscan 6 start call IN true elapsed dt; Q false; end call same true? Calling twice might add dt? No.\nCould avoid end call and instead delay trip initialization to next scan? Then clear one late.\n\nCould call TON only once at end based on new state and use Q after call for same-scan finalization. Let's design:\n- At scan start, heatTimer status from previous invocation persists.\n- Process:\n  - sensor fault update demand.\n  - if protection active prior: provisional output false. If maybe cooldown already Q from end of last scan? Since we finalize at end we clear same scan, so prior protection should not be active with Q true unless no demand? But we'd clear regardless per P10, so no.\n  - if no protection: if heatTimer.Q true and heater had been active (i.e. heat timer IN was true previous scan, Q true), trip.\n  - else desired output = valid demand.\n\n- Call cooldownTimer(IN := protection, PT:=T#3S) at end.\n- If protection was active before this call and cooldownTimer.Q now true, then per P10 clear same scan. Set protection false, then maybe output can restart same scan if valid demand. But can we rely on heatTimer? `heatTimer` wasn't called this scan yet maybe. Need call heat timer.\n- Call heatTimer with what and when? We can call heatTimer at end with new output, but trip detection based Q from prior call? Let's establish invocations.\n\nMaybe create own timing using `TON` isn't necessary. Could use `TIME()` / `LTIME()` and store timestamps. However, simulator's dt and CODESYS system time may align. Tone robust? Formal verify likely abstracts timers? Let's see verify tool.\n\nThe expected candidate likely simple TON based. Formal verifier perhaps supports TON auxiliary? It may reject UNSUPPORTED_ST if timers. Need test.\n\nLet's inspect tool via call compile. Need produce source, maybe include supporting timer? Standard TON. Try a candidate.\n\nLet's think candidate implementation with two TONs and exact semantics. Common PLC solution:\n\n```\nheatTimer : TON;\ncoolTimer : TON;\nsensorFault : BOOL;\nheatDemand : BOOL;\n\nCurrentTemperature := TemperatureSensorInput;\nSetTempDisplay := SetTemperature;\nsensorFault := TemperatureSensorInput < 0.0 OR TemperatureSensorInput > 100.0;\n\nIF NOT sensorFault THEN\n    IF TemperatureSensorInput < SetTemperature THEN\n        heatDemand := TRUE;\n    ELSIF TemperatureSensorInput > SetTemperature THEN\n        heatDemand := FALSE;\n    END_IF;\nEND_IF;\n\nheatTimer(IN := HeaterOutput, PT := T#5S);\nIF heatTimer.Q THEN\n    OverheatProtection := TRUE;\n    heatTimer(IN := FALSE);\nEND_IF;\n\nIF OverheatProtection THEN\n    coolTimer(IN := TRUE, PT := T#3S);\n    IF coolTimer.Q THEN\n        OverheatProtection := FALSE;\n        coolTimer(IN := FALSE);\n    END_IF;\nELSE\n    coolTimer(IN := FALSE, PT := T#3S);\nEND_IF;\n\nIF sensorFault OR OverheatProtection THEN\n    HeaterOutput := FALSE;\nELSIF heatDemand THEN\n    HeaterOutput := TRUE;\nELSE\n    HeaterOutput := FALSE;\nEND_IF;\nHeaterStatus := HeaterOutput;\n```\n\nIssue cooldown starts one scan after trip and clears one late. In above, trip scan calls heatTimer Q then sets protection. Then `IF OverheatProtection` calls coolTimer true, first eligible elapsed0. At trip+3s scans:\n- trip scan cool call elapsed0\n- next +dt=1\n- 2\n- trip+3 scan before call? At beginning of that scan, coolTimer.Q state from call at 2s, not 1s? At end of that scan call gives Q true but output not cleared until next scan. One late.\n\nCould after calling cool timer, if Q clear in same scan. But TON.Q updates immediately in same invocation. We can call and then test. Then:\n```\nIF OverheatProtection THEN\n   coolTimer(IN := TRUE, PT := T#3S);\n   IF coolTimer.Q THEN\n      OverheatProtection := FALSE;\n      coolTimer(IN := FALSE);\n   END_IF;\nEND_IF;\n```\nAt trip+3 scan, coolTimer call adds dt and returns Q true, clears same scan. Is that consistent with timing? call at trip scan elapsed 0. At scan +1 elapsed 1, +2 2, +3 3. Yes. So clear same scan if call and check. Then heat output may restart same scan if demand. Need heatTimer reset during protection. On clear branch set heatTimer(IN:=FALSE) perhaps. At beginning heatTimer call with IN previous HeaterOutput false, resets. If later output true, no timer call, so first energized sample considered current. Next scan call with output true starts timing? Wait earlier we assumed at next scan call with IN true updates by dt from previous invocation, making elapsed dt, not zero. But first eligible invocation should be current scan (when output true) elapsed zero. Since at end current scan we didn't call heatTimer with IN true, next scan call sees IN true but is first qualifying invocation? TON sees IN true now; prior call had IN false. On rising edge, standard TON likely elapsed zero, not dt. Good. But our code at start calls heatTimer using current HeaterOutput (previous scan) before output update. On restart same scan: at start timer IN false (previous output false) => resets. Then output true at end, no call. Next scan start IN true, rising edge -> elapsed 0. Great.\n\nBut for normal start, scan 0: start timer IN false resets, output true. scan1 start IN true -> first true call elapsed 0, not dt! If standard TON rising edge semantics yields elapsed 0. Then trip timeline shifts one scan: continuous energized time at scan1 should be dt1 (one interval since energized scan0), but TON gives 0. Unless simulator TON with IN true from prior scan uses dt since prior invocation even at rising edge. Need know expected.\n\nSpec says \"first energized scan at elapsed zero\", \"Timer elapsed time is zero on the first eligible sampled invocation; dt_ms is elapsed wall time since previous invocation\". If at scan0 output becomes true, that's first energized sample but timer wasn't invoked with IN true then. At scan1, elapsed since scan0 should be dt1, not zero, because scan0 is the first eligible. Thus timer needs to be invoked with IN true at scan0 (after output decision) or compute manually. In PLC scan, TON call at scan0 after output true would be first eligible elapsed zero; at scan1 call adds dt. So end-of-scan timer call is needed, and reading Q at next scan. Or call heat timer at start with previous output, which at scan0 previous false, so not eligible; but could manually add one dt? TON doesn't know previous output status. Could call heat timer at end with current output. Then first eligible scan0 elapsed0, scan1 adds dt1, ... scan5 adds dt5 => 5s, Q true at end scan5. We could trip at end scan5 same scan via checking Q. That yields trip at same scan when elapsed reaches 5s, correct. So best call timer after output decision and check immediately. But to know heatLimitReached before finalizing output, compute preliminary desired output, call heat timer, check Q, then override output. Need structure.\n\nLet's formulate:\nEach scan:\n1. sample outputs mirrors, fault, demand.\n2. call cooldown timer? At beginning based on protection prior? Better at end.\n3. Determine if protection currently active and whether clears at this scan using explicit timer call after? Could call cooldown at beginning based on prior protection, adds dt, returns Q; then if Q, set protection false immediately before output. On trip at end call cooldown true to initialize. But TON call twice in trip scan (begin false then end true) maybe okay, elapsed stays0. On active scans: beginning call IN true increments; if Q true clear before output. Then at end call IN false reset. Good. This matches:\n   - trip scan k=5: at beginning prior protection false -> cooldown(IN false) reset. Then final trip, end cooldown(IN true) elapsed0.\n   - k=6: beginning prior protection true -> cooldown(IN true) from previous call at k5, elapsed dt6=1s. If not Q remains.\n   - k=7: 2s.\n   - k=8: 3s -> Q true -> clear before output. Good.\nNo need end call after clearing? If set false, call cooldown(IN:=false) at end resets. Fine.\n4. heat timer: At beginning, could call with prior output? To detect trip for current scan at exactly threshold, update from previous. But restart same scan after cooldown clear: heat timer at beginning had IN prior output false (during protection) reset. Then if output restarts this scan, we can call heat timer again with true at end, initializing elapsed0. Need process.\nAt beginning heat timer with prior output and fault state: if prior output true, invocation adds dt, returns Q if reached threshold. If Q, trip before output. Good. At scan0: prior false, call false reset. Then decide output true, call at end with true -> first eligible elapsed0. At scan1: begin IN true, invocation adds dt1 => elapsed1. Good. At scan5 begin adds dt5=>5, Q true -> trip output false, protection true. End cooldown initialized.\nBut what if at scan5 the beginning heat timer call has Q true and then end we call heat timer again with false to reset? We can avoid second heat call; calling at end with IN false resets. Similarly protection active at start, call heatTimer false resets each scan. At trip scan beginning Q true, then final output false, end call false reset. Good.\nWhat if cooldown clears at scan8: beginning heatTimer IN previous false -> reset. If demand and valid, output true; end heatTimer true -> elapsed0. Good.\nSo two timer calls per scan? Actually one at beginning with prior output, one at end with final output. Calling TON twice per scan could potentially add dt twice? On non-rising, first call updates elapsed by dt, second call immediately maybe adds 0 if simulator uses actual time, but if simulator adds dt on every call, could double. Better use one call per scan at end, but need Q detection before final output and threshold in same scan. We can call once at end with provisional output and then correct output. But P6 says limit reached -> protection and !output in same scan. If Q only detected after end call, can then adjust. Let's do process with preliminary output, call heatTimer once at end with preliminary output, if Q then trip and output false. Then outputs finalized after timer call. That should work.\n\nDetailed ordering:\n- Compute sensor fault, demand, displays.\n- Clear protection if due (using cooldown timer once at start? Could call cooldown once per scan at start based on current prior protection; plus initialize on trip via direct elapsed? We could call cooldown at start; if trip at end, need start cooldown. Could on trip set a flag but next scan call cooldown with true would be first eligible elapsed0 and clear at +3s, but that's one scan late if trip initialized next scan. Could on trip call cooldown second time. Or set internal trip time manually).\nMaybe use one timer call per scan and carefully state.\n\nAlternative use IEC `TON` for both with calls at beginning and end only when state changes. Timer behavior when IN true and called at end after trip should start. Then next scans beginning? Could use one cooldown call per scan at end, and use `coolTimer.Q` from previous end to clear at beginning. As noted that's one scan late because Q from end previous scan at exactly 3s should have been observed at previous scan, but we did not act until next. Could after end call check Q and clear in same scan, then output again. That's one call. Great: implement decision provisional, end call timer, inspect Q, clear/trip and recompute output all same scan.\n\nLet's create a unified sequence with heat and cool timers called exactly once each per scan at end of logic, then perhaps final adjustment.\n\nSequence:\n1. sensorFault, demand, displays.\n2. Determine if protection should remain/clear using `coolTimer.Q` from prior invocation? We can instead call cool timer after provisional protection state? Let's define explicit stages and recalc.\n\nAt start:\n- `protectionActive := OverheatProtection` previous.\n- If protectionActive, provisional desired output false. Else provisional desired = valid & demand.\n- Need heatLimitReached based on continuous heating including current sample. If prior output true and this scan adds dt, then threshold. We can call heatTimer(IN := prior HeaterOutput? or provisional output?) after deciding provisional? Since no fault? Continuous energized time includes if previous output true up to now and desired remains true. If input now above setpoint, output should turn off, continuous heating ends at this scan; does elapsed reach 5s just as demand drops? P6 heatLimitReached definition: continuous energized time has reached 5s at current scan. At current scan, output not yet finalized. If we call timer with provisional output false due no demand, we would reset and not trip. But has continuous energized time reached 5s? At scan clock, prior heater was energized for 5s, but now demand cleared. Should overheat protection trip? \"If heater works continuously for more than ... trigger\". If at same scan temperature above setpoint, heater should turn off due no demand. Does protection trigger due prior 5s? Ambiguous. LTL P6 says G(heatLimitReached -> protection & !output). heatLimitReached based continuous energized time at scan. If it hits 5s exactly when demand goes false, should trigger? Since heater worked continuously for 5s and evaluation threshold >=5s, likely yes protection latches. But output would be off anyway. Then cooldown 3s. Could be expected. Need model timers independent of current demand: heatTimer IN should represent heater actually energized on previous scan, and add dt on current invocation. If current demand false, heater had worked for interval. At current scan, elapsed reaches 5s -> trip. Once trip, protection true. That seems faithful.\n\nThus call `heatTimer(IN := HeaterOutput)` using prior output at start (or at end before overwriting) and inspect Q. That adds dt if prior heater energized. Step at scan after previous energized. Since scan0 output false at call, then after setting output true we need initialize heat timer to elapsed0 for first energized sample. Could call again at end with true, but avoid double increment? If prior call IN false resets, second call true elapsed0. That's safe. For subsequent scans, call once at start with true and increments dt; then if final output remains true, if call again end would double maybe. Could avoid second call if already true. Only initialize at end when output transitions false->false? Specifically when provisional output is true and prior output false, call heat timer with true to set elapsed0. If prior output true, start call already updated; no second. If final output false, need reset timer at next scan; no need same scan? But if trip at current, after start Q true, at end output false; next scan start call false resets. The elapsed remains Q true for current but next reset. Fine. However if output false due fault and current scan start call IN true updates, next scan resets. Fine. Heat limit could trip same scan as fault? We must sensor fault override. If prior heater on, elapsed reaches 5 AND current fault, should protection trip or just fault? Requirements fault turns off and resets continuous time. Safety priority: sensor fault or active overheat overrides demand. Fault should reset continuous-heating time, no overheat trip likely. If heatTimer Q this scan based prior, but current fault means immediate off and reset; protection should probably not trip because continuous heating is invalid. Need avoid trip if sensorFault. If sensorFault then call heatTimer(IN:=false) resets and do not use Q.\n\nAt scan with above setpoint, no fault, prior output true, threshold reached: heatTimer Q true. If we choose not to trip because demand false, we violate? P6 atomic heatLimitReached true regardless. Formal spec likely expects trip. So trip even if demand false. That's safe.\n\nCall heat timer at start:\n```\nheatTimer(IN := HeaterOutput AND NOT sensorFault, PT:=T#5S);\nheatLimitReached := heatTimer.Q;\n```\nOn fault, IN false resets immediately and Q false. Good. If current above setpoint but no fault, IN uses prior output true => update and Q maybe. Good.\n\nThen protection logic:\n- If OverheatProtection currently true:\n   - call cooldownTimer? At start maybe `coolTimer(IN := TRUE...)` and inspect Q -> if Q clear; if not remains. On trip scan cooldown starts at end. Need cooldown initialization. Could use separate `coolTimer(IN := OverheatProtection, PT:=T#3S)` called at start based prior protection. On trip, call again at end true. That's two calls only in trip transition. If TON start elapsed0, okay. On active scans one call start. But if we call at start and Q becomes true, clear; then at end call with false resets. Good. On clear and restart same scan, heat timer start had IN prior output false reset; end initialize true. Perfect.\n- If no OverheatProtection:\n   - if heatLimitReached -> protection true, output false, initialize cooldown end.\n   - else output valid demand.\n\nBut at the trip scan, `coolTimer` at start was IN false, reset; then protect true; call at end with true -> elapsed0. Great.\nAt active scan start: cool timer IN true, updates dt and Q. At trip+3 scan Q true -> clear. Then output may restart.\nAt active scan where sensorFault etc, cooldown unaffected (per assumption fault doesn't clear/restart active cooldown). Calling cooldown true adds dt irrespective.\nAt trip scan with sensor fault? We avoid trip because heatTimer Q false due fault. fine.\n\nNow TON double-call issue: heat timer call at start and possible end init. For normal scan0, start false reset; end true init. For scan1, start true increments; no end call because prior prior output? We need know previous output before start. Store `wasHeating := HeaterOutput;` at beginning. If provisional final output true and not wasHeating, call end init. If wasHeating, no end. If final output false and wasHeating? call end false to reset? Not necessary but can to keep state consistent. Yet if trip Q true and final false, calling end IN false resets Q. That means `heatLimitReached` local was true but timer Q now false. P6 output protection true. Fine. Next scan start false. For output false due demand, reset in same scan. Safe. But calling TON a second time with IN false likely no time increment.\n\nFor cooldown similarly: start call based prior protection; if final protection state differs, call at end to initialize/reset. On trip state true from false -> end true. On clear state false from true -> end false reset. On unchanged no end. This seems robust.\n\nCould avoid calling TON at all at end on initialization by using TON? To set elapsed 0, call needed. Fine.\n\nFormal verification: Tool likely uses timed automata? It may not support TON. We can test. The formal specs names sensorFault/demand/heatLimitReached are atomic propositions possibly the verifier maps them to variables in candidate declaration? We need have internal booleans named exactly `sensorFault`, `demand`, `heatLimitReached`, `cooldownDone`, `protectPrev`? The specification formulas may not be lowered. The verifier tool probably maps. Need declarations include these names? Task interface doesn't specify internals. LTL uses ghost names. The verifier likely constructs environment variables itself. We may include internal flags. If our variables named heatDemand not demand, mapping maybe tool uses expression? Unclear. Better name internal BOOL `sensorFault`, `demand`, `heatLimitReached`, `cooldownDone`, `protectPrev` to help verification? But `protectPrev` is environment ghost not candidate. We can define internal booleans. Formal tool likely has translation conventions. Need see result.\n\nMaybe easiest use `TON` standard. But formal verification may require no timers or can symbolically model? Let's try compile with a candidate and then verify. We have 10 min maybe.\n\nWe need decide if tests. simulate_st likely needs cases format unknown. Could look at docs via tool? Only tool calls no directory. Tool schemas generic. We can craft cases perhaps common:\n```\n{\"cases\":[{\"name\":\"...\", \"steps\":[{\"inputs\":{\"TemperatureSensorInput\":..., \"SetTemperature\":...}, \"expected\":{...}, \"dt_ms\":1000}]}]}\n```\nTool accepts arbitrary. It may reject and reveal schema. We have 3 sim calls. Use later.\n\nFirst compile candidate. Need exact ST syntax CODESYS. Use `TON` from standard library. `T#5S`, `T#3S`. `ELSE` etc.\n\nPotential issue function block declaration name \"FB_Task\" and implementation only body. Supporting objects none (TON is std library). Need declaration syntax:\n```\nFUNCTION_BLOCK FB_Task\nVAR_INPUT\n    TemperatureSensorInput : REAL;\n    SetTemperature : REAL;\nEND_VAR\nVAR_OUTPUT\n    CurrentTemperature : REAL;\n    SetTempDisplay : REAL;\n    HeaterStatus : BOOL;\n    OverheatProtection : BOOL;\n    HeaterOutput : BOOL;\nEND_VAR\nVAR\n    sensorFault : BOOL;\n    demand : BOOL;\n    heatLimitReached : BOOL;\n    wasHeating : BOOL;\n    heatTimer : TON;\n    cooldownTimer : TON;\nEND_VAR\n```\nCan we have internal local named `demand`? Fine. `heatTimer : TON;` standard.\n\nBody:\n\n```\nCurrentTemperature := TemperatureSensorInput;\nSetTempDisplay := SetTemperature;\nsensorFault := (TemperatureSensorInput < 0.0) OR (TemperatureSensorInput > 100.0);\n\nIF NOT sensorFault THEN\n    IF TemperatureSensorInput < SetTemperature THEN\n        demand := TRUE;\n    ELSIF TemperatureSensorInput > SetTemperature THEN\n        demand := FALSE;\n    END_IF;\nEND_IF;\n\nwasHeating := HeaterOutput;\n\nheatTimer(IN := wasHeating AND NOT sensorFault, PT := T#5S);\nheatLimitReached := heatTimer.Q AND NOT sensorFault;\n\nIF OverheatProtection THEN\n    cooldownTimer(IN := TRUE, PT := T#3S);\n    IF cooldownTimer.Q THEN\n        OverheatProtection := FALSE;\n    END_IF;\nELSE\n    cooldownTimer(IN := FALSE, PT := T#3S);\nEND_IF;\n\nIF NOT OverheatProtection AND heatLimitReached THEN\n    OverheatProtection := TRUE;\nEND_IF;\n\nIF sensorFault OR OverheatProtection THEN\n    HeaterOutput := FALSE;\nELSIF demand THEN\n    HeaterOutput := TRUE;\nELSE\n    HeaterOutput := FALSE;\nEND_IF;\nHeaterStatus := HeaterOutput;\n\nIF HeaterOutput AND NOT wasHeating THEN\n    heatTimer(IN := TRUE, PT := T#5S);\nELSIF NOT HeaterOutput AND wasHeating THEN\n    heatTimer(IN := FALSE, PT := T#5S);\nEND_IF;\n\nIF OverheatProtection AND NOT was... \n```\nNeed track previous protection for cooldown init. Store `wasProtected := OverheatProtection` before logic.\nThen:\n```\nIF OverheatProtection AND NOT wasProtected THEN\n    cooldownTimer(IN := TRUE, PT:=T#3S);\nELSIF NOT OverheatProtection AND wasProtected THEN\n    cooldownTimer(IN := FALSE, PT:=T#3S);\nEND_IF;\n```\nBut at active scan start call cooldownTimer(IN:=TRUE) already added dt. At trip scan start call false reset, then end true init. Fine. At clear scan start true Q clear, end false reset. Good.\n\nBut `wasProtected` local. Likewise `wasHeating`. At initial scan outputs false.\n\nOrder issue: when active protection reaches cooldown Q at start, we set protection false. Then if heatLimitReached? heatTimer start was called with wasHeating false, so false. Output can restart if demand. Good.\n\nWhen no protection and heatLimitReached from start: set protection true. Then final output false. Cool init.\n\nWhen sensorFault with prior protection active: cooldown still progresses, may clear while fault. That's per assumption. Output false due fault. Good.\nWhen sensorFault occurs while prior heater on and timer Q potentially true: `heatTimer(IN := wasHeating AND NOT sensorFault)` resets and Q false, no trip. Good.\nDemand freezes on fault because update skipped. If previous demand false, stays false. On recovery, demand according comparison. If at equality after fault, retains previous frozen state. Good.\n\nEquality behavior: retain demand. In scan0 equality, demand initialized false. Good.\n\nP11 above setpoint -> output false. Our sensor valid, above sets demand false, output false. Yes.\nP1 fault -> false. yes.\nP2 protection -> false. yes.\nP3 status equals output. yes.\nP4 displays. yes.\nP5 permitted demand + valid + not protection + !heatLimit -> output true. Our if demand true. But `heatLimitReached` maybe false when no heating? yes. What if demand true and protection false but heatLimitReached false, output true. yes.\nP9 no demand -> false. yes.\nP6 heatLimitReached -> protection & false. Our heatLimitReached set on timer Q. Then if not already protection, set true before output. yes.\nP7 protection previous & !cooldownDone -> protection current. Our at start update cooldown; if not Q remains true. yes.\nP10 protectPrev & cooldownDone -> !protection. Our cooldown Q true clears same scan. yes.\nP8 fair time persistent demand resumes. yes after finite 3s.\n\nDoes heatTimer use elapsed at threshold as desired? At scan1 after scan0 output true, start call IN true. TON when IN true but previous call was also true? Previous call at end scan0 was true. So scan1 elapsed adds dt. yes. At scan5 5000, Q true. Great.\nBut calling heatTimer at start scan1 after previous end scan0 call: in a TON implementation, last call time at scan0 end. It computes elapsed += time since last call. In simulator, both calls within scan0 maybe same timestamp, second doesn't add. Fine.\nWhen scan0 start call IN false resets. End call true initializes. Good.\nAt scan where output transitions true->false due demand above, start call true adds dt, Q perhaps; then end call false reset. If Q true, protection trips. likely okay.\nAt scan where output transitions false->true after a no-demand period, heatTimer start call false resets, end true init. good.\n\nOne subtlety: Continuous heater time should reset on sensor fault. If sensor fault lasts, start call false resets Q. Good.\n\nAnother subtlety: Protection trip timestamp and cooldown. At trip scan, start cooldown false resets. End call true init. Next scans add dt. At trip+3s, at start cooldown call was already true from previous end (trip+2s) and adds dt = 3s, Q true -> clear before output. Good.\n\nHowever, if trip occurs and within same scan cooldown call true; then `cooldownTimer.Q` possibly true if PT? PT=3s, elapsed 0, false. good.\n\nNow P10 at trip+3s with persistent demand: OverheatProtection gets false and output true same scan. This is allowed. HeaterStatus true. cooldownDone should true. yes.\n\nPotential issue OverheatProtection output is latching but if sensor fault at exact clear scan, we clear then output false. P10 requires clear anyway. yes.\n\nNow candidate compile. Use compile_st.","sequence_number":3523}

