ith heat and cool timers called exactly once each per scan at end of logic, then perhaps final adjustment.\n\nSequence:\n1. sensorFault, demand, displays.\n2. Determine if protection should remain/clear using `coolTimer.Q` from prior invocation? We can instead call cool timer after provisional protection state? Let's define explicit stages and recalc.\n\nAt start:\n- `protectionActive := OverheatProtection` previous.\n- If protectionActive, provisional desired output false. Else provisional desired = valid & demand.\n- Need heatLimitReached based on continuous heating including current sample. If prior output true and this scan adds dt, then threshold. We can call heatTimer(IN := prior HeaterOutput? or provisional output?) after deciding provisional? Since no fault? Continuous energized time includes if previous output true up to now and desired remains true. If input now above setpoint, output should turn off, continuous heating ends at this scan; does elapsed reach 5s just as demand drops? P6 heatLimitReached definition: continuous energized time has reached 5s at current scan. At current scan, output not yet finalized. If we call timer with provisional output false due no demand, we would reset and not trip. But has continuous energized time reached 5s? At scan clock, prior heater was energized for 5s, but now demand cleared. Should overheat protection trip? \"If heater works continuously for more than ... trigger\". If at same scan temperature above setpoint, heater should turn off due no demand. Does protection trigger due prior 5s? Ambiguous. LTL P6 says G(heatLimitReached -> protection & !output). heatLimitReached based continuous energized time at scan. If it hits 5s exactly when demand goes false, should trigger? Since heater worked continuously for 5s and evaluation threshold >=5s, likely yes protection latches. But output would be off anyway. Then cooldown 3s. Could be expected. Need model timers independent of current demand: heatTimer IN should represen